sections.body:
[`calculateAffordability`](diff:shared/src/affordability.ts#L67-L99) finds the most expensive home whose monthly cost fits debt-to-income limits:

| Limit | Housing (front-end) | Housing + other debts (back-end) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Comfortable | 25% | 33% |
| Standard | 28% | 36% |
| Stretch | 31% | 43% |

The monthly budget is the tighter of the two. There's no closed form for the price because PMI changes in steps with the loan-to-value ratio, and taxes and insurance scale with the price. Monthly cost only ever rises with price, so the function binary-searches it to within 50 cents:

```ts
while (high - low > 0.5) {
  const mid = (low + high) / 2;
  if (costsAt(mid, input).total <= monthlyBudget) low = mid;
  else high = mid;
}
```

The upper bound is safe: every payment repays at least `loan / payments`, so a price above `downPayment + budget × payments` can't fit.

The result also reports which ratio was the limit, so the page can say whether income or existing debt is holding the price down.
